patience of those seeking to sate their  bloodlust and schlocky scares haggis  oozes final act is certain to haunt the  minds of any viewers who make it to the  end for years to come if history has  taught us anything it's that Hollywood  loves a good story of rebellious smart  whip youths running amuck what classics  like rebel without a cause a Clockwork  Orange and spring breakers have kept the  bar for such films raised perpetually  high over the years Tory Finley's  thoroughbreds proves that there's still  room for one of cinema's favorite  subgenres to grow at the center of  Finley's dark trauma tea is a pair of  upper-crust Connecticut teens who exist  on opposing ends of the emotional  spectrum Lily played by Anna Taylor joy  feels everything too strongly while  olivia cooks Amanda is incapable of  feeling anything like you don't have any  negative feeling like I don't have any  feelings ever when the estranged friends  are reunited they find that they're each  losing control of their lives and are  uniquely equipped to help each other out  that help comes at a cost as the girls  one shot at liberation involves a  deviously violent plan while it propels  thoroughbreds narrative forward it's  hardly the focal point of the film with  Finley instead using it to craft a  penetrating character study about a pair  of brilliant but tragically fractured  young minds horror movies are  essentially designed to take audiences  into the dark corners of the world and  show them the unspeakable things that  happen there sometimes that dark corner  is a physical place as in the Texas  Chainsaw Massacre other times it's a  place in the mind like the cell  Nickolas patches grisly debut the eyes  of my mother is the rare case where mind  in place converged to explore one of the  darkest corners ever  captured on film the film's opening  moments are surprisingly serene with a  girl in her family living in relative  peace on an isolated farm but pêche  takes a sledgehammer to that idealic  life early in the action when he does  the eyes of my mother goes dark in ways  that words simply cannot convey ways  that ensure the film will stay burned  into your brain for all eternity  we'd wager you've heard whispers of the  film's vivid depictions of violence  torture and dwara while the film shot in  stunning black and whites is unflinching  in its depiction of set jacks the most  gruesome deeds tend to occur off screen  oh that only heightens viewers reactions  besh further deepens the darkness by  using the fragile emotional astark iike  Maggie lays against viewers and service  of turning the eyes of my mother into a  character study about trauma and soul  consuming loneliness  speaking of soul-crushing loneliness  it's the main culprit at work in the  relentlessly bleak possession film the  black coats daughter directed by oz  Perkins the son of psychos Anthony that  being said there is a mysterious evil  force running amok in the film that may  or may not have claimed the soul of the  central character and forced her into  committing unconscious acts of violence  possible demons and possessions aside  loneliness and isolation are the real  driving forces at play in the black  coats daughter the film unfolds largely  on the grounds of an all-girls Catholic  school somewhere in the far distant  north when two of the girls played by  Karen and chip guy and Lucy Boynton are  stranded there over winter break things  get weird very quickly with the younger  girl apparently being possessed by some  unseen demonic presence meanwhile a  parallel narrative finds a grieving  couple picking up a desperate young  woman played by Emma Roberts at a bus  stop  not knowing she's an escaped mental  patient these narratives will eventually  converge in wickedly clever fashion and  to utterly heartbreaking effect Perkins  controls the action every step of the  way with a steady hand and singular  vision building a suffocating sense of  atmospheric thread into every frame at  the pill in doing so he delivers a  deeply humanist supernatural tragedy and  one of the darkest possession films  we've ever seen it's probably a bit  unfair to label Darren Aronofsky's  mother a horror film although there's a  palpable sense of unease running through  the film and patently horrific moments  throughout if we're being completely  honest mother doesn't fit neatly into  any single genre because there's never  really been a movie quite like it before  and we're still not sure how Aronofsky  got such a brazenly over-the-top  freakshow made we're glad he did though  because Aronofsky's frenzied tale of a  couple  Dilek existence being demolished by  increasingly bizarre occurrences from  the outside world is about as much DB  escanso fun as you can have in a panic  inducing movie even if we've come to  accept a certain level of button-pushing  from Aronofsky over the years mother  finds the director pushing boundaries  further than ever  in doing so he crafted a harrowing  cinematic allegory unlike any film  that's come before and likely anything  that will come after sometimes it's not  so much the story of film tells that  makes one uneasy as it's the characters  who populate it if you've already seen  Dan Gilroy searing new cycle satire  Nightcrawler you know full well that  Luke bloom is one of those characters  you also know that the reason the  character in the film itself is so  unsettling is because of the abrasive  energy Jake Gyllenhaal brings to the  role though Gyllenhaal's work in  Nightcrawler was ignored by the Academy  his turn as the morally empty bloom  remains not just one of the best  performances of that year but of his  career Nightcrawler may well be the best  film the actor has ever started and  undoubtedly the most disturbing the film  unfolds among the seedy underbelly of  Los Angeles and vines Jill and Paul's  bloom attempting to make a name for  himself in the world of freelance crime  journalism as he rises to fame bloom  begins to eradicate the line between  eyewitness to tragedy and willing  participant bolstered by crackling  writing and direction from Gilroy  Gyllenhaal's performances bloom  transforms Nightcrawler from satire into  a full-blown creep show about a modern  vampire with a troubling taste for  on-camera blood when I say that I want  these things I mean that I want them and  I don't want to have to ask again with  boldly stylistic flicks like pusher  drive and only God forgives Danish  filmmaker Nicolas Winding Ruffin has  proved himself to be a filmmaker wholly  obsessed with seedy pulp dramas dressed  up in stark neon drenched visuals though  it remains a divisive film even among  reference fanbase the neon demon is  essentially the culmination of  everything that's made repin such an  intriguing artistic force oddly enough  the neon demon also features one of  repens least compelling setups a pretty  young woman moves to LA in the hopes of  becoming a model the wickedness repens  spins from that simplistic setup is  nothing short of genre genius with the  city and everyone who runs it literally  feasted on the woman's youthful vitality  even as they wax poetic on what beauty  really is visually arresting a morally  fanciful and often gruesomely realistic  the neon demon is basically everything  Nicolas Winding reppin does  well turned up to 11 love it or hate it  the film remains one of the most deeply  jarring portraits of fashion culture the  movies have ever presented structured as  a near kaleidoscopic look at tragedies  past and present Len Ramsey's we need to  talk about kevin is a film less  concerned with the motivations of the  fateful act at its core a mass killing  in a school than it is with the lives  behind it as such the film focuses much  of its narrative energy on a mother  played by Tilda Swinton and her son  Kevin who will one day be the architect  of the unthinkable tragedy the film  follows the pair's troubled relationship  from birth and finds Swinton giving one  of the finest performances of her career  as a mother who desperately tries to  love her son even as his psychopathic  behaviors become more pronounced with  age  it's a perilous landscape with Ramsey  deftly walking the tightrope between  humanizing and demonizing Kevin even as  the film explores the tragic mother who  always saw the evil within  as the film jumps back and forth in time  exploring the aftermath of the violence  as much as the prelude a soul-shaking  sense of dread takes whole and we need  to talk about kevin becomes a fractured  mirror designed to reflect a sickness  consuming the world around us wisely  ramsay offers no remedy and instead  leaves us to wallow in the mess we as a  society seemed destined to keep cleaning  up check out one of our newest videos  right here plus even more looper videos  about your favorite movies are coming  soon subscribe to our YouTube channel  and hit the bell so you don't miss a  single one
 

